{{also|sea change}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|sea|change}}; sense 1 (“movement of people from cities to rural coastal areas; act of relocating from an urban to a rural coastal community”) was possibly coined by the Australian author and demographer {{w|Bernard Salt}} in his book ''The Big Shift'' (2001),<ref>{{cite-book|author=Bernard Salt|authorlink=Bernard Salt|title=The Big Shift: Welcome to the Third Australian Culture: The Bernard Salt Report|location=South Yarra, Vic.|publisher=Hardie Grant Pub.|year=2001|isbn=978-1-876719-29-6}}.</ref> based on the title of the Australian television series ''{{w|SeaChange}}'' (1998–2000 and 2019) about a lawyer who moved with her daughters from the city to a coastal town.<ref>{{cite-book|author=Bernard Salt|authorlink=Bernard Salt|chapter=Values and Mores|title=The Big Picture: Life, Work and Relationships in the 21st Century|location=Prahran, Vic.|publisher=Hardie Grant Books|year=2006|year_published=2007|section=part 1 (Life)|pages=22–23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_47PTPYk6m8C&pg=PA22|isbn=978-1-74066-528-5|passage=In the modern context ''SeaChange'' was the title of a popular Australian television program first screened in 1998. I then unashamedly commandeered the term in my first book ''The Big Shift'', published in 2001, to describe the shift by Australian people to the coast.}}</ref>

